{"id":954,"date":"2020-11-12T13:10:17","date_gmt":"2020-11-12T21:10:17","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=954"},"modified":"2024-09-01T20:10:29","modified_gmt":"2024-09-02T03:10:29","slug":"codrone-arduino-joystick-controls","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-arduino-joystick-controls\/","title":{"rendered":"CoDrone Joystick Controls"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215b0d0dfb\" name=\"6a2215b0d0dfb\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Throttle, yaw, pitch, and roll<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Air vehicles like your CoDrone move a little differently than land vehicles. While land vehicles can move forward, backward, and sometimes side-to-side, air vehicles can do all of that and move up and down. There are four words that you can use for your CoDrone&#8217;s flight direction: roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle. You&#8217;ll learn about each of them and how they work with your CoDrone in this lesson![\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215b0d0efa\" name=\"6a2215b0d0efa\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Roll<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Roll<strong>\u00a0<\/strong>is the horizontal, or side-to-side, tilt of the drone. Adding roll will make the drone tilt and fly to the right, and reducing roll will make the drone tilt and fly to the left.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;955&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215b0d0f4d\" name=\"6a2215b0d0f4d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Pitch<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Pitch<strong>\u00a0<\/strong>is the forward and backward tilt\u00a0of the drone. Adding pitch will make the drone tilt and fly forward, and reducing pitch will make the drone tilt and fly backwards.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;958&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215b0d0fa3\" name=\"6a2215b0d0fa3\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Yaw<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div class=\"activity2__rtf\">\n<p>Yaw<strong>\u00a0<\/strong>is the left and right rotation of the drone. Adding yaw will make the drone turn to the right, and reducing yaw will make the drone turn to the left.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;959&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215b0d1099\" name=\"6a2215b0d1099\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Throttle<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Throttle is the vertical, or up and down, motion of the drone. Adding throttle will make the drone fly higher, and reducing throttle will make the drone fly lower.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;960&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215b0d131b\" name=\"6a2215b0d131b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Using the remote<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]If you uploaded the default flight controller, these are the flight commands!<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Throttle is controlled by the left joystick\u2019s up and down motion.<\/li>\n<li>Yaw is controlled by the left joystick\u2019s right and left motion.<\/li>\n<li>Pitch is controlled by the right joystick\u2019s up and down motion.<\/li>\n<li>Roll is controlled by the right joystick\u2019s right and left motion.<\/li>\n<li>Stop<strong>\u00a0<\/strong>is controlled by covering the left IR sensor (bt1).<\/li>\n<li>Landing<strong>\u00a0<\/strong>is controlled by the right IR sensor (bt8).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Pro tip: add buzzer commands so you can hear when an IR sensor is used![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;961&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_single_image image=&#8221;962&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_single_image image=&#8221;963&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 7 Throttle, yaw, pitch, and roll [vc_column_text]Air vehicles like your CoDrone move a little differently than land vehicles. While land vehicles can move forward, backward, and sometimes side-to-side,&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-arduino-joystick-controls\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1752,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"easy","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":804,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[],"lesson-tag":[2164,2165,2115,2177,2111],"class_list":["post-954","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-codrone","lesson-tag-codrone-pro","lesson-tag-flight-directions","lesson-tag-getting-started","lesson-tag-remote-control","module-getting-started","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/ar